  <h1>From Idea to Online: Build a Small Project One Step at a Time</h1>
  <p>A big idea can feel exciting and overwhelming at the same time. The way forward is to make the first version small enough to finish. You don't need a perfect brand, dozens of features, or an expensive tool stack to test whether an idea is useful.</p>

  <h2>1. Define the person and the problem</h2>
  <p>Write one sentence: “This project helps [person] do [specific task] more easily.” If that sentence is vague, narrow the audience or problem before you start building.</p>

  <h2>2. Choose the smallest useful version</h2>
  <p>List everything you might add, then identify the one feature that proves the idea. For a website, that might be a clear homepage, one useful article, and a working contact link. Save the extras for later.</p>

  <h2>3. Use AI as a collaborator</h2>
  <p>AI can help outline a page, brainstorm headlines, explain code, draft social posts, or build a checklist. Give it real constraints and review the output. Verify factual claims, test code and links, and replace generic statements with your own experience.</p>

  <h2>4. Publish, then test</h2>
  <p>Ask a few people to try your project on a phone and a computer. Can they understand what it does? Can they find the main action? Do the links work? Fix the biggest friction point before adding features.</p>

  <h2>5. Keep a short improvement list</h2>
  <p>Sort feedback into three groups: “fix now,” “consider later,” and “not needed.” Make one improvement at a time and keep a copy of the last working version. A small project you can maintain is more valuable than an ambitious project you abandon.</p>

  <h2>The takeaway</h2>
  <p>Creation is a cycle: make a useful first version, put it in front of people, learn, and improve. Progress comes from finishing the next step—not planning every possible step at once.</p>

